WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.290
Updates are essential for IoT lifecycle
maintenance.

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.949
Devices may need fixes, configuration
changes, dependency updates,

3
00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:09.577
certificate transitions, feature
adjustments,

4
00:00:09.657 --> 00:00:12.204
or support improvements years after
deployment.

5
00:00:12.284 --> 00:00:14.771
The update path changes what the device
will run,

6
00:00:14.851 --> 00:00:17.520
so it deserves careful design and repeated
testing.

7
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.543
Signed update concepts help a device
decide whether an update is authorized.

8
00:00:21.623 --> 00:00:25.262
The design should also include version
policy, compatibility checks,

9
00:00:25.342 --> 00:00:27.647
metadata validation, storage assumptions,

10
00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:31.367
and a clear point where an update is
considered ready for installation.

11
00:00:31.447 --> 00:00:35.693
This course stays at the defensive design
level and does not implement update code.

12
00:00:35.773 --> 00:00:37.774
Staged rollout is part of fleet safety.

13
00:00:37.854 --> 00:00:41.433
A team may release to a small group,
monitor health, expand gradually,

14
00:00:41.513 --> 00:00:44.182
and pause if diagnostics show unexpected
behavior.

15
00:00:44.262 --> 00:00:48.326
Firmware should provide enough version and
health information to support that

16
00:00:48.406 --> 00:00:50.651
workflow without exposing sensitive data.

17
00:00:50.731 --> 00:00:52.672
Power-loss behavior must be planned.

18
00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.724
Devices may lose power during download,
staging, commit,

19
00:00:55.804 --> 00:00:57.320
or first boot after an update.

20
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.100
The system should define what state is
durable, what state is temporary,

21
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.002
how incomplete work is detected, and how
the device returns to a known path.

22
00:01:05.082 --> 00:01:07.629
Rollback and recovery policy should be
explicit.

23
00:01:07.709 --> 00:01:11.895
Some products need to return to a previous
valid image after failed health checks.

24
00:01:11.975 --> 00:01:14.644
Others may restrict older versions based
on policy.

25
00:01:14.724 --> 00:01:17.574
Either way, the behavior should be
documented, tested,

26
00:01:17.654 --> 00:01:19.474
and reflected in support runbooks.

27
00:01:19.554 --> 00:01:22.951
Fleet lifecycle includes field repair and
end-of-life planning.

28
00:01:23.031 --> 00:01:27.034
Teams should know how devices are
repaired, replaced, retired, transferred,

29
00:01:27.114 --> 00:01:28.752
or disconnected from services.

30
00:01:28.832 --> 00:01:31.501
Dependency maintenance, credential
replacement,

31
00:01:31.581 --> 00:01:35.463
and support ownership should be planned
before the first large deployment.

32
00:01:35.543 --> 00:01:36.878
Negative tests are vital.

33
00:01:36.958 --> 00:01:40.961
Exercise invalid metadata, incompatible
versions, interrupted downloads,

34
00:01:41.041 --> 00:01:45.044
reset during commit, failed health checks,
full storage, and recovery flows.

35
00:01:45.124 --> 00:01:49.067
Update reliability is a security
requirement because a device that cannot be

36
00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:52.180
maintained safely becomes harder to
operate responsibly.
